# the argument of descartes-real-distinction.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Descartes, Discourse on Method, part IV. The whole argument is one sentence (S1).
# "Could suppose" is read as conceivability and given plain atoms, not modal operators.
atom	can_suppose_no_body	I could suppose that I had no body, and that there was no world nor any place in which I might be
atom	can_suppose_not_exist	I could suppose that I was not
atom	think	I thought to doubt of the truth of other things
atom	exist	I was (I existed)
atom	reason_believe_exist	I would have reason to believe that I existed
atom	essence_thinking	I am a substance whose whole essence or nature consists only in thinking
atom	independent_body	that it may exist, I have need of no place, nor am dependent on any material thing
atom	distinct	the mind by which I am what I am is wholly distinct from the body
atom	survives_without_body	although the body were not, the mind would still continue to be all that it is
P1	S1	can_suppose_no_body
P2	S1	¬can_suppose_not_exist
P3	S1	think → exist
P4	S1	think
P5	S1	¬think → ¬reason_believe_exist
# The bridge from what I can and cannot suppose to what my essence is never appears in the text.
# The "thence concluded" plainly relies on it.
A1	-	can_suppose_no_body ∧ ¬can_suppose_not_exist ∧ (¬think → ¬reason_believe_exist) → essence_thinking ∧ independent_body
C1	S1	essence_thinking ∧ independent_body	from P1 P2 P5 A1
# "so that ... wholly distinct ... would still continue to be" is drawn from C1 with no stated further premise.
C	S1	distinct ∧ survives_without_body	from C1
